This file describes the directives and concepts tested by this test set.

test set name: gpio02

directives:

  rtems_gpio_virtual_register
  rtems_gpio_virtual_raise

  and, through them, the generic GPIO directives gpio01 covers:

  rtems_gpio_get_info
  rtems_gpio_pin_get_info
  rtems_gpio_pin_by_name
  rtems_gpio_pin_configure
  rtems_gpio_pin_get_configuration
  rtems_gpio_pin_release
  rtems_gpio_pin_get
  rtems_gpio_pin_set
  rtems_gpio_pin_toggle
  rtems_gpio_pin_get_multiple
  rtems_gpio_pin_set_multiple
  rtems_gpio_pin_irq_enable
  rtems_gpio_pin_irq_disable

concepts:

  - A pin of this controller is a pair of callbacks, and a consumer drives
    it with the ordinary directives without knowing that.

  - Capabilities are per pin, not per controller: one table holds a proxy
    pin that interrupts, a clock gate that only drives, and a readiness
    line that only reads.

  - A pin with no set callback reports no RTEMS_GPIO_CAP_OUTPUT, so an
    output configuration on it is refused with ENOTSUP by the generic
    layer.  Nothing in the driver has a special case for it.

  - A table that claims a capability no callback backs is refused at
    registration rather than reported with the capability dropped.

  - A proxy pin forwards to a second controller through the same
    directives an application uses.  Its callbacks may do that because the
    second controller is a second mutex; a callback that came back to its
    own controller would deadlock, which is why the header says so.

  - Whether a call may block is the registrant's answer: the controller
    with the proxy pin says true and the one behind it says false.

  - An output whose pin does not report RTEMS_GPIO_CAP_OUTPUT_READBACK
    reads back the last level written, and its get callback is not called.
    One that does report it is asked every time.  So a clock gate needs no
    get callback to be readable.

  - RTEMS_GPIO_FLAG_ACTIVE_LOW is the generic layer's and the callback
    sees the physical level, asserted against what the callback was handed
    rather than against a second call of the API.

  - rtems_gpio_virtual_raise() delivers a pin's interrupt synchronously in
    the context that called it, including from inside a set callback,
    which is how a software signal makes its own write the event.

  - A bulk operation touches no word past the caller's word_count, and a
    bulk write naming one pin that cannot be written writes none of them.

negative control:

  Each check below was run with the code it tests broken, and the line the
  test then stopped on is recorded.  A test that has never been seen to
  fail is not evidence that the code it covers works.

    1. gpio-virtual.c, rtems_gpio_virtual_check_pin(): remove the check
       that RTEMS_GPIO_CAP_OUTPUT has a set callback behind it.
       -> init.c:292, reached from test_registration_is_checked().  The
          table registered and the pin reported an output it cannot drive.

    2. gpio-virtual.c, rtems_gpio_virtual_reads_shadow(): ignore
       RTEMS_GPIO_CAP_OUTPUT_READBACK and always read the shadow.
       -> init.c:563 "value == 1", from test_readback().  A change made
          behind the driver went unseen on a pin that promises to see it.

    3. gpio-virtual.c, rtems_gpio_virtual_pin_configure(): mark the pin in
       use before applying the level rather than after.
       -> init.c:273 "errno == expected_errno", from
          test_callbacks_are_called().  A configure the set callback
          refused left the pin in use, so the read that should have been
          refused with EBADF was not.

    4. gpio-virtual.c, rtems_gpio_virtual_pin_set_multiple(): check each
       pin in the loop that writes it instead of in a pass before.
       -> init.c:707 "soft_gate.sets == 0", from test_bulk().  The call
          still returned ENOTSUP and one pin had already been written.

    5. gpio-virtual.c, rtems_gpio_virtual_bitmap_pins(): walk the
       controller's pin count instead of the caller's words.
       -> init.c:693 "soft_high.gets == 0", from test_bulk().  A pin in
          the second word was read from a one word bitmap.

    6. gpio-virtual.c, rtems_gpio_virtual_raise(): take the handler of pin
       0 rather than of the pin raised.
       -> init.c:761, from test_interrupts().  The raise reported ENOENT
          for a pin that had a handler.

    7. gpio-virtual.c, rtems_gpio_virtual_write(): invert the level in the
       driver as well as in the generic layer.
       -> init.c:492 "soft_gate.level == 0", from
          test_callbacks_are_called().  The callback was handed the
          opposite of the level the caller asked for.
